Certainly! Below is a LaTeX code snippet using TikZ and pgfplots to create a professional-looking 2D plot as per your specifications. The plot features a point labeled \( x \) being dominated by three other points labeled \( y_1 \), \( y_2 \), and \( y_3 \). The region to the top right of \( x \) is shaded pale red, and axis-parallel dashed lines in gray pass through the point \( x \).

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usepackage{pgfplots}
\pgfplotsset{compat=1.17}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        width=0.8\textwidth,
        height=0.6\textwidth,
        axis lines=middle,
        xlabel=$x$,
        ylabel=$y$,
        xmin=0, xmax=10,
        ymin=0, ymax=10,
        xtick={0,2,...,10},
        ytick={0,2,...,10},
        xticklabels={\empty},
        yticklabels={\empty},
        grid=both,
        grid style={dashed,gray},
        grid options={xmin=0,xmax=10,ymin=0,ymax=10},
        enlargelimits=false,
        clip=false,
        ]

        % Define professional colors
        \definecolor{palered}{RGB}{255,192,192}
        \definecolor{grayline}{RGB}{169,169,169}

        % Plot points
        \addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {
            (4,5) % Point x
            (2,3) % Point y1
            (6,7) % Point y2
            (8,4) % Point y3
        };

        % Shade the region to the top right of x
        \addplot[fill=palered, draw=none, fill opacity=0.3] coordinates {
            (4,5)
            (10,5)
            (10,10)
            (4,10)
        } -- cycle;

        % Axis-parallel dashed lines through x
        \addplot[grayline, dashed] coordinates {
            (4,0) (4,10)
            (0,5) (10,5)
        };

        % Labels
        \node[above right] at (4,5) {$x$};
        \node[above left] at (2,3) {$y_1$};
        \node[above right] at (6,7) {$y_2$};
        \node[below right] at (8,4) {$y_3$};

    \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

