To achieve this, you can use HTML and CSS to position the SVG icon to the right of the input field and make it clickable. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. **HTML Structure**: Place the input and the SVG icon in the same container.
2. **CSS Styling**: Use CSS to position the SVG icon to the right of the input.
3. **JavaScript**: Add an event listener to the SVG icon to trigger the `onClick` function.

Here's an example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with Clickable Icon</title>
    <style>
        .input-container {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }
        .input-container input {
            padding-right: 30px; /* Space for the icon */
        }
        .input-container .icon {
            position: absolute;
            right: 0;
            top: 0;
            bottom: 0;
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 20px; /* Adjust size as needed */
            height: 20px; /* Adjust size as needed */
            background: url('path/to/your/icon.svg') no-repeat center center;
            background-size: contain;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="input-container">
        <input type="text" placeholder="Type something...">
        <div class="icon" onclick="myFunction()"></div>
    </div>

    <script>
        function myFunction() {
            console.log("Input function called!");
            // Add your function logic here
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
- **HTML**: The `input-container` div wraps both the input and the icon. The icon is placed absolutely to the right of the input.
- **CSS**: The `.input-container` ensures the input and icon are in the same line. The `.icon` is positioned absolutely to the right of the input, and its size and background are set to display the SVG.
- **JavaScript**: The `onclick` attribute on the icon calls `myFunction()` when clicked.

Replace `'path/to/your/icon.svg'` with the actual path to your SVG file. Adjust the sizes and styles as needed for your design.

